<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-16" top="0.5in" left="0.5in" right="0.5in" bottom="0.5in">
    <title>Lecture Worksheet - Resonance and Transients</title>

    <!-- Exercises start here. -->
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The general solution to
                <m>\ddot{x}+\gamma\dot{x}+\omega_{0}^{2}x=\cos(\Omega t)</m> can
                be written as
                <md>x(t)=a(\Omega)\cos(\Omega t+\phi(\Omega)) + Ae^{-\gamma t/2}\cos(\omega_{0} t) + Be^{-\gamma t/2}\sin(\omega_{0} t)</md>,
                where <m>a</m> and <m>\phi</m> were found in class.
            </p>

            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            What two pieces of information do we need to
                            determine the constants <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>?
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Write the system of equations that determines
                            <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> for the case
                            <m>x(0)=\dot{x}(0)=0</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Go to <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/31qlfilhjl">this Desmos calculator</url>
                            and play around.
                            The curve <m>y_{1}(t)</m> is the solution with the
                            transient, and <m>y_{2}(t)</m> is the steady-state
                            solution (both rescaled so you can change the
                            frequency and damping)
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Look at <m>y_{1}</m> and <m>y_{2}</m> together.
                            How does the time it take for the black curve to
                            match the red curve depend on the damping?
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Now show only <m>y_{3}</m>, which is the steady
                            state solution normalized to have an amplitude of 1
                            at resonance.
                            Keep <m>\omega_{0}</m> constant and vary the driving
                            frequency <m>\Omega</m> ( <m>w_{1}</m> on Desmos).
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            You should find that the amplitude of <m>y_{3}</m>
                            is only noticeable if you are close to resonance.
                            How does the range of frequencies depend on the
                            damping?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <p>
            This week has been all about the equation <m>\ddot{x}+\gamma\dot{x}+\omega_0^2x=f_0\cos(\omega t)</m>,
            corresponding to a damped oscillator driven by a single sinusoidal driving force.
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Using what you know about solutions to this differential equation, 
                    what do you think the steady state solution <m>x(t)</m> would look like if instead of a 
                    single driving force you had two driving forces of different frequencies
                    <m>\omega_1</m> and <m>\omega_2</m>? 
                    That is, what is a particular solution to
                    <m>\ddot{x}+\gamma\dot{x}+\omega_0^2x=a\cos(\omega_1 t)+b\cos(\omega_2t)</m>? 
                    Note, extensive derivations are not required here: you can use results you learned in class/textbook.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Go to desmos and plot the following functions:
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f(x) = \sin(x)+\frac{\sin(3x)}{3}+\frac{\sin(5x)}{5}+\frac{\sin(7x)}{7}+\frac{\sin(9x)}{9}+...</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>g(x) = \cos(x)-\frac{\cos(2x)}{4}+\frac{\cos(3x)}{9}-\frac{\cos(4x)}{16}+\frac{\cos(5x)}{25}+...</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Challenge: can you turn the expressions on our Desmos calculator into functions that you can use to find the response
                    <m>x(t)</m> of the oscillator to the above interesting driving forces?
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>